WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.290
Welcome everyone, to
today's CIFAR VIRTUAL TALK

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:09.110
in our series on the theme
"Science is the exit strategy".

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:12.890
CIFAR is a Canadian based
global charitable organization

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:16.280
that convince extraordinary
minds to address the most

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:19.840
important questions facing
science and society.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:23.300
We connect researchers with
experts in government, industry

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.760
and civil society on
timely and relevant issues,

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.610
to develop new ways of
thinking and to promote change

00:00:29.610 --> 00:00:31.044
and policy and practice.

00:00:31.044 --> 00:00:34.343
I'm Rebecca Finlay and
it is my pleasure to host

00:00:34.343 --> 00:00:39.343
today's talk on government
responses to COVID-19,

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:43.050
economics, politics and the
role of scientific advice,

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.230
with Professor Tim Besley.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:49.038
Tim is a fellow in the CIFAR
program on institutions

00:00:49.038 --> 00:00:53.800
organization and growth,
and school professor of

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.470
Economics of Political
Science and W.Arthur Lewis,

00:00:57.470 --> 00:00:59.830
Professor of Development Economics

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:02.190
at the London School of Economics.

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:06.750
He studies how institutions
shape economic policy decisions,

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.720
with particular emphasis
on improving the quality

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:13.660
of policy making and reducing instability.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:17.490
He has written extensively
on an impressive range

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:21.030
of important topics in political economy.

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:25.050
No surprise my favorite
judging by the title alone

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:28.097
is a forth coming co-authored publication,

00:01:28.097 --> 00:01:32.320
"Gender Quotas and the
Crisis of the Mediocre Man".

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.050
Tim has held several
prominent policy roles,

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:37.030
including as an external member of

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:39.990
the Bank of England's
monetary policy committee.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:43.670
In the UK 2018 New Year's
honors he was knighted

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:47.770
for his services to
economics, and public policy.

00:01:47.770 --> 00:01:51.300
Today, Tim will discuss
the subtleties of providing

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:55.590
science advice to government
during a pandemic, the timely

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:57.270
an important topic indeed

00:01:57.270 --> 00:02:00.270
and we are honored to have him with us.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:02.420
A few housekeeping notes before we begin,

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:04.960
today's session will last 30 minutes.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.640
If you have a question,
please submit it through

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:10.900
the Q&amp;A function at the
bottom of your screen.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:13.340
Finally, this session is
being recorded and will be

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.140
available on our website next week.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:18.413
Tim, thank you for joining us.

00:02:18.413 --> 00:02:21.170
- Thank you Rebecca, its a Pleasure.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:22.890
- Great to have you here.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:26.360
Let's start with a more general
question to frame today's

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:28.330
discussion as we move forward.

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:31.865
What is political economy
and how does it inform

00:02:31.865 --> 00:02:34.890
your thinking around the role

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.213
of providing scientific
advice to government?

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:41.614
- So political economy is a
field that sort of emerged

00:02:41.614 --> 00:02:44.257
in the mainstream of economics,
at least the way that

00:02:44.257 --> 00:02:47.724
I'm gonna talk about it
today in the last 25 years,

00:02:47.724 --> 00:02:50.690
trying to understand not just what good

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:54.000
economic policy advice is,
but how you actually get

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.370
good economic policy outcomes.

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.774
So it studies the institutional
frameworks in which policy

00:02:58.774 --> 00:03:03.774
advice is given and tries to
understand what it is about

00:03:05.410 --> 00:03:09.530
the institutions of government
that allow the best advice

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:12.020
to shine through, it's not
enough for economists to say

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:16.390
do X or Y, they have to frame
that in a context where it's

00:03:16.390 --> 00:03:19.130
the politicians who are
gonna act and own the policy.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:22.794
And so it's really about
studying that whole process

00:03:22.794 --> 00:03:27.403
of economic policy formation
and its widest sense.

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:32.150
- Great, so you've
recently written a column

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:34.230
with one of your colleagues
on Andr'es Velasco

00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:35.387
and it's titled,

00:03:35.387 --> 00:03:38.774
"Politicians can't hide
behind scientists forever,

00:03:38.774 --> 00:03:41.000
even in a pandemic".

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.377
And in it you have a great line it's

00:03:43.377 --> 00:03:46.580
"Politics uninformed by science

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:50.690
quickly becomes quackery,
but science, unmediated

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:54.190
by politics is of limited
use when it comes to solving

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:57.230
a collective action problems
such as a pandemic".

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:58.950
Can you talk a little bit about this

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:01.100
and how this relates
directly to the experience

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:02.833
we've been having within COVID-19?

00:04:04.007 --> 00:04:06.170
- Yeah, well, that's a sort of line that

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:10.650
I think very much came out of
our study of global economy,

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:13.680
in the sense of scientific advice,

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:17.250
whether it's science from
medics, or epidemiologists

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:19.343
or whomever or economic advice,

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:23.760
has to be effective if it's going

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.130
to find its way into policy.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:27.970
And of course, we hope the policy makers

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:30.160
will listen to that advice.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:32.765
But we can't forget that
at the end of the day,

00:04:32.765 --> 00:04:37.080
it's the policy makers
who have to own an advice,

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.720
whether it be in scientific
or economic advice,

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:42.400
and act upon it in the way they judge.

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:45.260
I mean, you cannot
displace or try to displace

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:47.490
the judgment of the political process.

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:50.410
We live in democratic
settings, a number of us

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:53.660
for very good reasons, because
we believe that politicians

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:56.610
should be held to account for
the decisions that are made.

00:04:56.610 --> 00:05:01.610
That there's a need for
politicians to make hard choices,

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:06.190
if there's a trade off between
some gaining and some losing

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:09.470
from a particular policy,
ultimately, the legitimacy

00:05:09.470 --> 00:05:14.360
for that trade off comes from
the political process itself.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.680
And what I don't think
scientists or any kind of experts

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:20.529
can do is to characterize
themselves as somehow standing

00:05:20.529 --> 00:05:24.880
above the political process,
as if they have some sort of

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:29.230
Olympian judgment that is not
available to those reelect.

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:31.804
It's really about working
with the process of realizing

00:05:31.804 --> 00:05:35.900
you have to convince the
politicians to act and you have

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:39.870
to provide the advice in a
balanced and reasonable way,

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:42.710
not pretending that things
are necessarily all going

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:44.475
in one direction very
often it's about explaining

00:05:44.475 --> 00:05:46.980
the pros and cons.

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:50.959
But ultimately, I think it
should it should enable politics,

00:05:50.959 --> 00:05:54.345
but not displace politics.

00:05:54.345 --> 00:05:57.418
- Yeah, I think that's
a really interesting

00:05:57.418 --> 00:06:02.243
area to dig into, and I think
it speaks to this question

00:06:02.243 --> 00:06:06.610
of trust in institutions, and governance,

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:11.060
and the roles of institutions
in society and how they play

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:15.331
and can complement, but also
conflict with each other.

00:06:15.331 --> 00:06:19.000
I was reflecting on the work
that we did here at CIFAR with

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:22.330
our expert advisory group on
society, technology and ethics

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:25.510
in the pandemic, when it was
clear that we were trying to

00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:28.810
balance the technological possibilities of

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:32.390
contact tracing apps and
other noble technologies

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:36.090
and the requirements for
governance and essentially,

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:38.680
the establishment and
commitment to a sustainable

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:40.983
public trust, without public trust,

00:06:40.983 --> 00:06:43.520
the apps would essentially fail.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:48.300
But trust in institutions
it's a hard thing to measure

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:49.740
and to understand.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:52.500
Can you talk a little bit about
that and what you think may

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:55.730
have been some of the
impacts of the pandemic,

00:06:55.730 --> 00:06:58.010
on the trust and in the
different institutions

00:06:58.010 --> 00:06:59.260
we have in our societies?

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:03.029
- Yeah, and let me add a
couple things, people often say

00:07:03.029 --> 00:07:05.684
trust is harder and easily lost as well.

00:07:05.684 --> 00:07:09.910
And I think that often
reflects some of what

00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:12.830
we experience, but I think
in the pandemic, it's been

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:15.790
particularly important the
extent of reliance we have

00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:20.200
on compliance with various
measures that have been taken,

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:23.410
and how much trust we
place in the wider populace

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:26.940
to follow what might be
called sensible advice rather

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:29.210
than thinking everything has to be solved

00:07:29.210 --> 00:07:31.100
through rules and regulations.

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:33.370
So it's kind of getting
that balance right between

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:36.063
what you trust citizens
to do and what you trust

00:07:36.063 --> 00:07:39.420
government to do is absolutely central.

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:43.140
And I think what I've been
struck by both with my experience

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:47.020
in the pandemic, but also
reflecting on other countries,

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:49.449
is how the institutional
framework tries to get

00:07:49.449 --> 00:07:51.350
that balance right.

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:53.270
And there are a number of
dimensions I think that are worth

00:07:53.270 --> 00:07:56.727
remarking on, one is the kind
of transparency of the advice

00:07:56.727 --> 00:07:58.272
I mean, Where's it coming from?

00:07:58.272 --> 00:08:00.780
On what basis has it been formed?

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:05.780
Is the evidence base a solid
as some people would claim.

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:10.202
And it's important to I
think, be straight with people

00:08:10.202 --> 00:08:14.560
about that not to over claim
what evidence can show,

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:16.670
particularly in areas
where the evidence is less

00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:20.160
than clear cut, and you build
trust, I think with a certain

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:24.780
degree of humility and honesty,
but you can't, in the end,

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:26.860
throw your arms up and say,
well, we don't know anything

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:28.360
that's not helpful either.

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:31.750
It's kind of trying to get
at what I would regard as,

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:35.409
a reasonable default position
and position you can defend

00:08:35.409 --> 00:08:38.650
short of evidence moving you away from it,

00:08:38.650 --> 00:08:41.340
and I think the art of it,
and you can get to this in

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:46.070
different ways is to hit
upon a default option

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:50.530
that is supportable by evidence
and you don't feel compelled

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:53.680
to keep shifting ground coz
if you go to the wrong place,

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:56.709
and evidence is repeatedly
coming up to say you're

00:08:56.709 --> 00:08:59.062
in the wrong place, it
will undermine trust again,

00:08:59.062 --> 00:09:02.320
in the whole system, not
just in that specific

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:05.020
piece of advice, and
so as much as possible,

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:07.220
you try and get those default positions

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:09.593
right as quickly and early as possible.

00:09:10.690 --> 00:09:12.780
- A little bit about what we've seen

00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:16.060
over the last several months with COVID-19

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:18.580
is that there's quite a dynamic nature

00:09:18.580 --> 00:09:22.545
to that interplay as the evidence changes,

00:09:22.545 --> 00:09:27.000
when the health advice evolves.
and we see that tension

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:30.264
between providing science
advice and the role of,

00:09:30.264 --> 00:09:34.380
the politician and the
institutions of government.

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:38.120
So there's a really interesting
dynamic around this rapid

00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:42.130
response mechanism, and I wanted
to talk to you a little bit

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:45.100
about DELVE, which is a group
that's been put together

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:47.119
that you are a member of in the UK,

00:09:47.119 --> 00:09:49.420
led by the Royal Society.

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:50.820
I think DELVE stands for

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:54.300
Data Evaluation and Learning
for Viral Epidemics,

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:57.300
if I look at my notes, and it's really

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:01.770
trying to apply data
evaluation and synthesis,

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:04.960
to different approaches
that have been taken.

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:07.520
And it's an interdisciplinary
group as well,

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:12.520
but quite rapidly developing
this, knowledge and information

00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:15.090
into government on the
actions that move forward.

00:10:15.090 --> 00:10:17.200
Can you talk a little bit
about how that has worked

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:18.930
and what you think some of the benefits

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:19.763
of that might have been?

00:10:19.763 --> 00:10:21.930
And maybe potentially some of the risks?

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:25.890
- Yeah, so many ways, DelVE
is a reflection of what

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:29.820
CIFAR mentor is because it's
trying to be issue driven

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:33.670
and bring expertise, regardless
of discipline together

00:10:33.670 --> 00:10:37.090
based around issue, so I felt
quite at home being a part

00:10:37.090 --> 00:10:40.490
of that even though as an
economist, I'm in a small minority

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:43.554
that disciplines many disciplines
involved Epidemiology,

00:10:43.554 --> 00:10:47.370
Public Health, Medical
Sciences of different kinds.

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:50.620
So it's been a very
interesting experience trying

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:55.620
to bring a group of people
together, to work on problems

00:10:56.657 --> 00:10:59.610
and to produce reports
which we hope will be useful

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:04.560
to informing policy, an example
would be the report we did

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:07.100
on "Testing Tracing and Isolation".

00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:09.940
Another one on the "Use of
Face Masks" as well as other

00:11:09.940 --> 00:11:12.200
reports in process.

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:14.438
So trying to sort of look
at the different approaches,

00:11:14.438 --> 00:11:16.700
disciplines take to evidence,

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:18.329
what kind of evidence they value.

00:11:18.329 --> 00:11:21.190
Let me give you an example, I
don't think in a pandemic like

00:11:21.190 --> 00:11:23.320
this you can put too much weight on

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:26.320
Randomized Control Trials,
not because they're not useful

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:28.970
when you can have them but
many of the pieces of advice

00:11:28.970 --> 00:11:31.893
you're giving simply don't
have an RCT evidence base.

00:11:31.893 --> 00:11:35.303
So if you said, well, we're
only going to do those things

00:11:35.303 --> 00:11:38.758
that are proven by
Randomized Control Trials,

00:11:38.758 --> 00:11:41.960
we're certainly not gonna
get very far when the degree

00:11:41.960 --> 00:11:45.500
of uncertainty and lack of
appropriate knowledge exists.

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:48.233
So it's kind of then working
together and saying, Well,

00:11:49.522 --> 00:11:51.016
what evidence is persuasive
and also getting a wider

00:11:51.016 --> 00:11:55.816
perspective on the evidence
to what extent do we wanna

00:11:55.816 --> 00:11:59.295
bring the economic cost
in alongside some of the

00:11:59.295 --> 00:12:02.520
cost to infection, you
know, there's been this

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:05.020
Balancing Act, which I'm
sure you're familiar with

00:12:05.951 --> 00:12:07.913
in Canada, of between trying
to get the public health right

00:12:07.913 --> 00:12:10.503
and trying to get the economics right.

00:12:10.503 --> 00:12:15.503
without, compromising
either too much, and again,

00:12:16.102 --> 00:12:19.928
through conversations within a
multidisciplinary environment

00:12:19.928 --> 00:12:23.060
we can sort of start to
speak each other's language

00:12:23.060 --> 00:12:25.570
or understand each other's
language, but ultimately,

00:12:25.570 --> 00:12:28.730
the view is we need to put
out there the best thinking

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:32.430
that we can find and, then input that

00:12:32.430 --> 00:12:33.763
into the policy process.

00:12:35.049 --> 00:12:37.570
- Yeah, that's very helpful.

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:41.430
I think the interesting approach
to look out to different

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:43.930
jurisdictions so I often
think about this is one of

00:12:43.930 --> 00:12:46.681
the things that CIFAR finds
really valuable in our process

00:12:46.681 --> 00:12:49.661
is having research programs
that are made up of

00:12:49.661 --> 00:12:53.805
senior academic leaders
who are based in different

00:12:53.805 --> 00:12:56.150
institutions in different
disciplines and in different

00:12:56.150 --> 00:12:58.322
countries and being able
to bring that together.

00:12:58.322 --> 00:13:01.810
We were able to hold an
international consultation

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:05.330
with several individuals based
in the UK as part of the work

00:13:05.330 --> 00:13:09.270
we did on the society technology
and ethics report as well.

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:11.370
How did you find that reaching into

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:14.210
your international networks
understanding what's happening

00:13:14.210 --> 00:13:17.084
outside of the UK to then
be able to provide advice

00:13:17.084 --> 00:13:19.023
into the UK government?

00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:22.723
- Well, I think goes without
saying I'm sure you know

00:13:22.723 --> 00:13:26.612
that we are all academics
typically embedded

00:13:26.612 --> 00:13:29.147
in international networks,
we're all a bit frustrated

00:13:29.147 --> 00:13:32.430
that we're housebound now
and can't be participating in

00:13:32.430 --> 00:13:35.180
international conferences,
I think zoom has provided

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:38.380
a platform at least
for us to stay in close

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:40.810
communication with our academic colleagues

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:42.910
around the world because
most of our disciplines

00:13:42.910 --> 00:13:44.882
are highly internationalized,
and therefore,

00:13:44.882 --> 00:13:47.710
we are all the time trying to look

00:13:47.710 --> 00:13:51.633
and see where the best knowledge is.

00:13:51.633 --> 00:13:56.200
Even though, working in a
country context as we are,

00:13:56.200 --> 00:13:59.909
we're trying to feed into
the UK policy response.

00:13:59.909 --> 00:14:04.890
Much of the time, it's what
have we learnt from country X

00:14:04.890 --> 00:14:07.540
or Y about how they approached it?

00:14:07.540 --> 00:14:10.310
And can we feed that in?

00:14:10.310 --> 00:14:11.390
What are the experts saying?

00:14:11.390 --> 00:14:13.450
Why are they saying potentially
something different?

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:17.040
Is there a justifiable reason

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:18.390
for taking a different position?

00:14:18.390 --> 00:14:20.630
Does it just reflect the
cultural institutions

00:14:20.630 --> 00:14:21.463
of another country?

00:14:21.463 --> 00:14:24.863
So when it's constantly trying
to synthesize and understand

00:14:24.863 --> 00:14:28.983
those different perspectives
to try and kind of work out

00:14:28.983 --> 00:14:31.722
what to conclude from that it's more,

00:14:31.722 --> 00:14:36.190
I'm slightly speaking against
the title of the (murbles).

00:14:36.190 --> 00:14:38.907
It's more of an art than
a science, I would say,

00:14:38.907 --> 00:14:41.430
being able to sift the
evidence and get that right.

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:43.760
But at the end of the day,
the science is a big input

00:14:43.760 --> 00:14:45.113
into the art if you like.

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:49.260
- Fair enough, that makes a lot of sense.

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:52.156
So one area that you and
I've just touched on briefly,

00:14:52.156 --> 00:14:55.550
was around the role of the
private sector, so there are many

00:14:55.550 --> 00:14:58.415
actors and an economy all of
them have different roles.

00:14:58.415 --> 00:15:02.730
Can you talk a little
bit about the role that

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:06.480
you see of the firm's
and the private sector

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:08.710
as part of the pandemic response?

00:15:08.710 --> 00:15:09.917
So we think about public health

00:15:09.917 --> 00:15:11.880
and the public health
response, we think about

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:15.440
economic stimulus and
innovation as a form of recovery

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:17.112
out of the pandemic.

00:15:17.112 --> 00:15:21.250
But what have you seen in
terms of the dynamics around

00:15:21.250 --> 00:15:24.408
the private sector engaging
with the government

00:15:24.408 --> 00:15:28.133
or being part of the
public health response?

00:15:29.090 --> 00:15:31.080
- There's a couple of things
on that I'd like to say,

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:34.019
one is the sort of direct
involvement in the private sector

00:15:34.019 --> 00:15:37.860
trying to deliver a
kind of recovery phase,

00:15:37.860 --> 00:15:40.129
there is little doubt
that we will have to get

00:15:40.129 --> 00:15:43.640
the private sector back into
some kind of decent shape

00:15:43.640 --> 00:15:45.840
to provide the jobs and
livelihoods for people

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:48.572
who've been affected by
the pandemic, but equally

00:15:48.572 --> 00:15:50.940
there will be a different world out there.

00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:53.150
One where business as
usual will not be possible

00:15:53.150 --> 00:15:55.160
in many sectors of the economy,

00:15:55.160 --> 00:15:56.720
at least for the foreseeable future,

00:15:56.720 --> 00:16:01.400
we hopefully get a vaccine
or more effective treatment.

00:16:01.400 --> 00:16:05.807
And so it's a case of
trying to work with business

00:16:05.807 --> 00:16:10.060
to make the best of what
is a difficult situation.

00:16:10.060 --> 00:16:12.235
For example, what kind
of testing protocols

00:16:12.235 --> 00:16:14.770
does one want in place?

00:16:14.770 --> 00:16:16.410
How does one encourage them as a matter of

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:18.680
voluntary compliance or compulsion?

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:21.510
I think we all think that in
the end, trying to get people

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:23.710
to do things voluntarily is advantageous,

00:16:23.710 --> 00:16:25.530
but that's not always feasible.

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:27.740
So you have to decide
where does the line lie

00:16:27.740 --> 00:16:32.220
between regulation and
voluntarism, but many firms

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:33.840
that particularly those
that want to get back

00:16:33.840 --> 00:16:36.573
to normal business have stepped
up and started voluntarily

00:16:36.573 --> 00:16:38.759
to engage in public health measures.

00:16:38.759 --> 00:16:41.890
Not entirely frivolous example is

00:16:41.890 --> 00:16:46.070
the professional soccer to
you football to us leagues

00:16:46.070 --> 00:16:48.900
which have basically in order
to be able to be allowed

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:52.290
to play again, have put in
very rigorous testing regimes

00:16:52.290 --> 00:16:53.653
for players and staff.

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:57.090
Because they realize that
was the only route back

00:16:57.090 --> 00:16:59.740
to some kind of normal
operation of their business,

00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:02.160
even though in fact, all
the fixtures have played

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:04.175
behind closed doors.

00:17:04.175 --> 00:17:06.020
So that's one aspect, the other aspect,

00:17:06.020 --> 00:17:09.620
which I will mentioned briefly
is the provision of data.

00:17:09.620 --> 00:17:14.570
So we've been very clear in this pandemic,

00:17:14.570 --> 00:17:17.149
what a wealth of intelligence
and information resides

00:17:17.149 --> 00:17:20.160
with the private sector, whether they'd be

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:22.226
mobile phone companies that
are helping us to put up

00:17:22.226 --> 00:17:26.330
tracing apps or whatever,
through financial firms

00:17:26.330 --> 00:17:28.430
that can have a much
more up to date and up to

00:17:28.430 --> 00:17:32.100
the minute picture of the
impact of the pandemic

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:35.150
on the economy, so one
needs to form sort of

00:17:35.150 --> 00:17:37.910
public-private partnerships to both access

00:17:37.910 --> 00:17:40.900
and analyze data, now raises
quite a few issues not least

00:17:40.900 --> 00:17:44.860
around data privacy, and
so it's not altogether

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:47.570
straight forward to know how you do that.

00:17:47.570 --> 00:17:50.540
But it has been, I think,
an interesting feature

00:17:50.540 --> 00:17:53.760
of the pandemic, that
need to forge those strong

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:58.620
partnerships, and together
to try and do the best

00:17:58.620 --> 00:18:01.470
and I think some of this
will outlive the pandemic,

00:18:01.470 --> 00:18:04.980
but new ways of looking at the
economy through the lens of

00:18:04.980 --> 00:18:08.710
the data that the private
sector has, or looking at

00:18:08.710 --> 00:18:11.250
public health measures through
the same lens will become

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:14.372
something which we may
say in the end, we want

00:18:14.372 --> 00:18:17.594
to maintain this, this
important for creating

00:18:17.594 --> 00:18:20.953
more effective intervention
and better societies.

00:18:22.060 --> 00:18:23.470
- That's a really interesting point.

00:18:23.470 --> 00:18:26.540
I think we've certainly seen in Canada,

00:18:26.540 --> 00:18:30.190
that the wealth of data
that we have within

00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:33.943
our health systems, many of
them at the provincial level,

00:18:35.350 --> 00:18:37.840
really, a really renewed understanding

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:39.880
of the value of the data

00:18:39.880 --> 00:18:44.290
and the capacity for it to inform research

00:18:44.290 --> 00:18:48.260
and decision making, you
know, both in terms of when it

00:18:48.260 --> 00:18:50.370
doesn't work well, and then, in terms of

00:18:51.431 --> 00:18:52.950
public health surveillance
and otherwise, but then also,

00:18:52.950 --> 00:18:55.729
when, really the benefit in thinking about

00:18:55.729 --> 00:19:00.729
how do we sustain and support
those dated infrastructures

00:19:01.060 --> 00:19:04.001
in a way that will allow the research

00:19:04.001 --> 00:19:06.700
and work that needs to be done?

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:08.770
It's a whole other question
when you think about

00:19:08.770 --> 00:19:10.950
the private sector, and
as you rightly admit,

00:19:10.950 --> 00:19:13.020
you have all sorts of constraints,

00:19:13.020 --> 00:19:15.624
consent based privacy policies
and otherwise that exist

00:19:15.624 --> 00:19:17.060
around that data.

00:19:17.060 --> 00:19:21.740
So what does that mean in
terms of thinking about how

00:19:21.740 --> 00:19:26.270
to integrate that or not into
government systems, as well.

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:29.360
And I think that will be one of I agree,

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:31.940
I think that is going to
be one of the legacies

00:19:31.940 --> 00:19:34.700
that will come out of this
time will be some understanding

00:19:34.700 --> 00:19:36.927
about what data and what
systems we need to have

00:19:36.927 --> 00:19:39.910
within our government
structures, and then also

00:19:41.290 --> 00:19:44.290
what the role of the private
sector might be in that regard.

00:19:45.430 --> 00:19:47.490
We've got one question from the audience

00:19:47.490 --> 00:19:50.850
that I want to drill into a
point you made earlier, Tim,

00:19:50.850 --> 00:19:55.560
around public trust and transparency.

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:58.630
And the question is
really, that oftentimes

00:19:58.630 --> 00:20:02.150
a politician will say that their advice

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:05.120
is based on science, but
they may not in fact,

00:20:05.120 --> 00:20:08.050
indicate where they got
the scientific advice from,

00:20:08.050 --> 00:20:11.300
or what the evidence might
be to support that advice.

00:20:11.300 --> 00:20:15.114
So how does the way in which
a politician communicates

00:20:15.114 --> 00:20:18.440
about the science and the evidence base

00:20:18.440 --> 00:20:19.728
for their information.

00:20:19.728 --> 00:20:23.500
How does that affect the
trust that citizens have

00:20:23.500 --> 00:20:26.288
in that information and in that advice?

00:20:26.288 --> 00:20:28.920
- I think this has been a
very interesting question,

00:20:28.920 --> 00:20:32.110
because this has been, I
think, one of the major areas

00:20:32.110 --> 00:20:37.110
where we've been learning as
we go through the pandemic,

00:20:37.540 --> 00:20:42.020
in many countries,
scientists in particular,

00:20:42.020 --> 00:20:44.170
who would not have even
been known to the public

00:20:44.170 --> 00:20:45.720
have become sort of public figures

00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:49.210
because politicians in particular,

00:20:49.210 --> 00:20:51.696
have found it convenient
to put them on pedestals

00:20:51.696 --> 00:20:55.910
and to have them directly
communicate with the public

00:20:55.910 --> 00:20:59.274
in order for them to try
and answer questions,

00:20:59.274 --> 00:21:02.063
concerns that the public have.

00:21:03.377 --> 00:21:07.650
So, sometimes instead of the
politician being the mouthpiece

00:21:07.650 --> 00:21:08.850
for this as the advice.

00:21:08.850 --> 00:21:13.839
If you ever tune in to the
broadcasts we have in the UK

00:21:13.839 --> 00:21:18.420
the press conferences, you'll
very often find the politician

00:21:18.420 --> 00:21:21.190
will hand off the question
directly to a scientist

00:21:21.190 --> 00:21:22.610
and say, this is one for the scientists

00:21:22.610 --> 00:21:23.590
now please answer them.

00:21:23.590 --> 00:21:26.210
There's no way, the scientists
can't hide at that point

00:21:26.210 --> 00:21:28.240
because they either state
an opinion or they don't.

00:21:28.240 --> 00:21:31.771
So then you can't just say a
scientist believes, you really

00:21:31.771 --> 00:21:34.180
figure out who's saying what.

00:21:34.180 --> 00:21:37.500
But I think there's a sort of
what wider background concern

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:40.130
and this is why it's been
evolving through the pandemic.

00:21:40.130 --> 00:21:44.660
Because initially the main
scientific body in the UK SAGE,

00:21:44.660 --> 00:21:46.790
there was not even any transparency

00:21:46.790 --> 00:21:49.230
about who sat on that body.

00:21:49.230 --> 00:21:52.417
And then at one point, they
were forced to publish the names

00:21:52.417 --> 00:21:57.417
and as time goes by people are
asking for more information.

00:21:58.190 --> 00:22:01.350
Maybe they should publish the minutes.

00:22:01.350 --> 00:22:03.970
For those of us who used
to work in Central Banking

00:22:05.155 --> 00:22:05.988
will be familiar with this.

00:22:05.988 --> 00:22:07.580
We used to publish our
central bank minutes

00:22:07.580 --> 00:22:10.070
after every policy decision to communicate

00:22:10.070 --> 00:22:12.080
to the public why we
were reaching a decision.

00:22:12.080 --> 00:22:14.780
But now that may become a feature

00:22:14.780 --> 00:22:17.600
of these more institutionalized
scientific bodies

00:22:17.600 --> 00:22:20.070
that they have to publish minutes

00:22:20.070 --> 00:22:22.941
and then maybe they also have
to publish some of the sources

00:22:22.941 --> 00:22:25.580
for those minutes in
which particular studies

00:22:25.580 --> 00:22:28.660
because as we know, it's
rarely that you have

00:22:28.660 --> 00:22:30.630
just a single study that's going

00:22:30.630 --> 00:22:33.050
to convince you to shift policy.

00:22:33.050 --> 00:22:35.170
That if that's really
what the policy people

00:22:35.170 --> 00:22:36.780
should be honest about that.

00:22:36.780 --> 00:22:38.741
What was the evidence that was decisive?

00:22:38.741 --> 00:22:42.339
And so this has been a
very interesting area

00:22:42.339 --> 00:22:44.620
to watch evolving.

00:22:44.620 --> 00:22:46.050
Countries will learn from each other.

00:22:46.050 --> 00:22:48.040
They'll look at what systems are in place

00:22:48.040 --> 00:22:51.980
and decide whether they want
the more that the system

00:22:51.980 --> 00:22:54.760
in which the politicians are
put more on the pedestal less

00:22:54.760 --> 00:22:57.416
on the pedestal quoted not quoted.

00:22:57.416 --> 00:23:00.971
But I think because this is all
hit us in such a short time,

00:23:00.971 --> 00:23:03.700
with very much little learning on the job

00:23:03.700 --> 00:23:05.033
as far as that goes.

00:23:06.280 --> 00:23:07.420
- Absolutely.

00:23:07.420 --> 00:23:11.230
So really interesting question
around the politicization

00:23:11.230 --> 00:23:12.690
of science advice.

00:23:12.690 --> 00:23:15.580
So we've seen, for example,
in the US around face masks

00:23:15.580 --> 00:23:17.588
and face masks wearing that there's

00:23:17.588 --> 00:23:20.390
that that's become a
rather partisan issue.

00:23:20.390 --> 00:23:24.100
So what advice would
you give to scientists

00:23:24.100 --> 00:23:26.700
who were providing policy
advice and to government around

00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:30.930
how to sort of stay away from
or to avoid the politicization

00:23:30.930 --> 00:23:32.903
of some of the advice they're providing?

00:23:32.903 --> 00:23:36.370
- Well, the sort of one
level of politicization

00:23:36.370 --> 00:23:39.400
that I think is in the hands
of the individuals concerned,

00:23:39.400 --> 00:23:41.050
and that's the extent to which they want

00:23:41.050 --> 00:23:42.570
to speak out or not against

00:23:42.570 --> 00:23:45.070
what is palpably government policy.

00:23:45.070 --> 00:23:47.245
I think once you do that,
you've kind of crossed the line

00:23:47.245 --> 00:23:49.590
and you have in it,
whether you like it or not

00:23:49.590 --> 00:23:51.390
become part of the political process.

00:23:51.390 --> 00:23:54.880
And I think that's always
been an issue that scientists

00:23:54.880 --> 00:23:56.570
or economists or anyone else face

00:23:57.726 --> 00:23:58.980
once you become oppositional,

00:23:58.980 --> 00:24:02.320
you do become part of
the political process.

00:24:02.320 --> 00:24:05.220
But I think the way
kind of more generally,

00:24:05.220 --> 00:24:07.440
I think about this is to
come back to this idea

00:24:07.440 --> 00:24:11.645
of a default policy that
I think there's sort

00:24:11.645 --> 00:24:13.470
of two stances you can take.

00:24:13.470 --> 00:24:16.097
One is that this policy
is contravening your view

00:24:17.641 --> 00:24:20.090
of what the evidence
says is a good policy.

00:24:20.090 --> 00:24:22.690
That's a very strong
statement, versus saying,

00:24:22.690 --> 00:24:25.690
I can't provide compelling evidence

00:24:25.690 --> 00:24:26.640
to move from this policy.

00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:28.597
I'm not sure it's right or wrong.

00:24:28.597 --> 00:24:32.390
So I think there's a sort of
difference between saying,

00:24:32.390 --> 00:24:35.700
we know this is wrong versus
we don't have sufficient

00:24:35.700 --> 00:24:37.550
evidence to move away from that.

00:24:37.550 --> 00:24:39.770
And I think it's when you cross that line

00:24:39.770 --> 00:24:41.700
into yes, I know this is
wrong and you're willing

00:24:41.700 --> 00:24:44.001
to step so publicly that you become a part

00:24:44.001 --> 00:24:46.120
of the political process.

00:24:46.120 --> 00:24:48.270
But very often science or economics

00:24:48.270 --> 00:24:49.547
or anything else you study

00:24:49.547 --> 00:24:53.240
is not so clear cut that
you can denounce something

00:24:53.240 --> 00:24:55.520
as evidently a bad policy.

00:24:55.520 --> 00:24:57.520
So a good example is the face masks.

00:24:57.520 --> 00:24:59.077
I just mentioned this
briefly because I think early

00:24:59.077 --> 00:25:03.620
on in the pandemic, the
view should probably

00:25:03.620 --> 00:25:06.564
have been taken that wearing face masks

00:25:06.564 --> 00:25:10.368
in confined spaces was the right policy.

00:25:10.368 --> 00:25:12.410
That wasn't the policy we took in the UK.

00:25:12.410 --> 00:25:15.040
I don't know for sure what you
what you decided in Canada.

00:25:15.040 --> 00:25:16.730
Now it's not because I could prove

00:25:16.730 --> 00:25:19.050
or anyone else could prove
that wearing a face mask

00:25:19.050 --> 00:25:20.730
is such a great idea.

00:25:20.730 --> 00:25:24.320
I mean, you can certainly had
to believe it's a bad idea.

00:25:24.320 --> 00:25:27.760
But it's certainly not evidently proven

00:25:27.760 --> 00:25:29.630
that it's a good idea.

00:25:29.630 --> 00:25:32.483
So that shouldn't become
the default policy.

00:25:33.560 --> 00:25:35.240
That once you take the default policy

00:25:35.240 --> 00:25:38.970
to be you shouldn't wear face masks,

00:25:38.970 --> 00:25:41.003
you shift about burden of proof.

00:25:42.436 --> 00:25:44.320
And that's a very different
proposition, which again,

00:25:44.320 --> 00:25:46.174
I think it's very hard to prove so.

00:25:46.174 --> 00:25:49.160
So I think making those
choices about where you think

00:25:50.217 --> 00:25:51.290
the presumption lies can
make a lot of difference

00:25:51.290 --> 00:25:53.616
to the way the public debate evolves.

00:25:53.616 --> 00:25:57.180
- Okay, we have just a few minutes left,

00:25:57.180 --> 00:26:00.970
but I just would love
for you to take a moment

00:26:00.970 --> 00:26:04.820
because clearly, providing policy advice

00:26:04.820 --> 00:26:07.030
has been a part of your academic career

00:26:07.030 --> 00:26:08.663
right from the very earliest
days, you've been involved

00:26:08.663 --> 00:26:11.817
in many different forms of
providing policy advice.

00:26:11.817 --> 00:26:13.860
And I wondered if you
could just sort of reflect

00:26:13.860 --> 00:26:16.102
on that I think there'd
be a lot of researchers

00:26:16.102 --> 00:26:18.690
on this talk, who would
be interested to hear.

00:26:18.690 --> 00:26:22.190
Why was that important
to your academic career?

00:26:22.190 --> 00:26:23.430
And if there was an experience

00:26:23.430 --> 00:26:24.980
that was particularly rewarding.

00:26:24.980 --> 00:26:25.813
What was it?

00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:29.732
- So I think the time
that it dawned on me more

00:26:29.732 --> 00:26:33.560
than any other time was
when I was a policymaker.

00:26:33.560 --> 00:26:34.870
You mentioned I was in the Central Bank

00:26:34.870 --> 00:26:37.090
during the Financial Crisis.

00:26:37.090 --> 00:26:40.730
And, of course there I was
in effect a policymaker

00:26:40.730 --> 00:26:43.639
taking part in the
response to that crisis,

00:26:43.639 --> 00:26:46.354
only in a narrow sense of the
monetary policy at the time.

00:26:46.354 --> 00:26:48.842
But a couple of things occurred to me

00:26:48.842 --> 00:26:52.253
that allow me to reflect on your question.

00:26:53.329 --> 00:26:57.050
That was just three years of my life,

00:26:57.050 --> 00:26:58.330
but all the rest of my life,

00:26:58.330 --> 00:27:00.090
I've been essentially an academic.

00:27:00.090 --> 00:27:02.790
But what was interesting
about that experience

00:27:05.319 --> 00:27:06.420
in terms of my academic life was realizing

00:27:06.420 --> 00:27:08.850
that there's two things
that I think academics

00:27:08.850 --> 00:27:10.930
can really contribute.

00:27:10.930 --> 00:27:13.780
And that's often to provide
a framework for thinking

00:27:13.780 --> 00:27:17.480
about the policy issues, which
while it won't necessarily

00:27:17.480 --> 00:27:22.370
resolve the issue, because the
issue is itself very complex,

00:27:22.370 --> 00:27:25.502
you can by framing it in
a in a particular way,

00:27:25.502 --> 00:27:28.537
make transparent to those
who have to make decisions,

00:27:28.537 --> 00:27:31.660
kind of what the pros and cons are broadly

00:27:31.660 --> 00:27:33.633
of taking different stances
and where the evidence

00:27:33.633 --> 00:27:35.960
would definitely say you
shouldn't do something

00:27:35.960 --> 00:27:38.580
versus where it doesn't prove
you shouldn't do something,

00:27:38.580 --> 00:27:40.800
but it might be prudent to do it.

00:27:40.800 --> 00:27:43.096
So I think one of the things that I've

00:27:43.096 --> 00:27:48.096
always found rewarding if you
like, engagement in policy

00:27:49.530 --> 00:27:53.220
is where you think you
can make a contribution

00:27:53.220 --> 00:27:58.220
by framing an issue in a particular way.

00:27:58.680 --> 00:28:02.400
So I think that, that's a
valuable part of what we do

00:28:02.400 --> 00:28:07.400
and link to that, academics
can take a long term view.

00:28:07.940 --> 00:28:10.803
I mean, even in the pandemic, where we're,

00:28:13.448 --> 00:28:14.323
we know we got to make
policy this week, next week,

00:28:15.442 --> 00:28:16.613
probably every week for
the next six months.

00:28:17.600 --> 00:28:19.400
I still think lifting your
head off a bit and thinking

00:28:19.400 --> 00:28:22.544
about the long term implications
is incredibly important.

00:28:22.544 --> 00:28:27.544
And if you get too sucked
into the day to day policy,

00:28:27.680 --> 00:28:31.027
you often kind of start to lose
sight of the bigger picture.

00:28:31.027 --> 00:28:33.920
Where is it we're going
and why are we going?

00:28:33.920 --> 00:28:36.030
And I think that's a very valuable asset

00:28:36.030 --> 00:28:38.347
that academics probably
sometimes underestimate.

00:28:38.347 --> 00:28:41.180
Now, admittedly, you can't
say I'll come back in 20 years

00:28:41.180 --> 00:28:42.520
and give you an answer
when you're in the middle

00:28:42.520 --> 00:28:44.741
of a pandemic but I think still being able

00:28:44.741 --> 00:28:46.700
to do slightly longer term thinking

00:28:46.700 --> 00:28:48.273
is really very, very important.

00:28:49.320 --> 00:28:52.640
- Absolutely critical and in a great spot

00:28:52.640 --> 00:28:55.200
for us to conclude today's discussion.

00:28:55.200 --> 00:28:56.550
Thank you so much, Tim.

00:28:56.550 --> 00:28:59.531
That's all the time we have for today.

00:28:59.531 --> 00:29:02.010
So before everyone signs off,

00:29:02.010 --> 00:29:03.940
I'd like to thank all
of you for joining us.

00:29:03.940 --> 00:29:05.410
And I'd especially like to thank you,

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:07.490
Tim, for your insights today.

00:29:07.490 --> 00:29:10.020
Extremely, extremely interesting
and useful conversation.

00:29:10.020 --> 00:29:11.630
Thank you so much.

00:29:11.630 --> 00:29:15.310
I'd also like to thank Rachel
Parker for her inspiration

00:29:15.310 --> 00:29:16.940
and organization of today's talk,

00:29:16.940 --> 00:29:19.380
as well as Daniel Batch
Anello and Jackie Sullivan

00:29:19.380 --> 00:29:21.740
in the proverbial control room.

00:29:21.740 --> 00:29:24.780
Please visit our website
cifar.ca to find out more

00:29:24.780 --> 00:29:26.539
about this series interview
recording of this talk,

00:29:26.539 --> 00:29:30.370
